<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#painted-grey-ware-culture">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Painted Grey Ware culture</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1200–600 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century-bce">13th century BCE</a> · Also known as Painted Grey Ware</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Painted Grey Ware culture is an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> archaeological culture of the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges plain</a> and the Ghaggar-Hakra valley in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, dated to roughly 1200–600 BCE. It is named after its fine grey pottery painted with black geometric patterns. Its villages, which used iron tools and grew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, barley and wheat, are associated with the Later Vedic period and with the Kuru and Panchala kingdoms.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/painted-grey-ware-culture.jpg" alt="Painted Grey Ware culture" width="320" height="237" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Avantiputra7 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Painted_Grey_Ware_Culture_(1200-600_BCE).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
